  "text": "Sure! Let's break down the task:\n\n### **Goal:**\nWrite a function `word_histogram` that takes a string as input and returns a dictionary where:\n- Each key is a **word** from the string (case-insensitive).\n- Each value is the **frequency** of that word.\n- **Punctuation is ignored** (so words like \"Hello,\" become \"hello\").\n\n---\n\n### **Steps to Solve:**\n1. **Convert the string to lowercase** to make the comparison case-insensitive.\n2. **Remove punctuation** using `string.punctuation`.\n3. **Split the string into words** (using spaces as delimiters).\n4. **Count the frequency** of each word using a dictionary.\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Here's the code:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert the text to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation\n    text = text.translate(str.maketrans('', '', string.punctuation))\n    \n    # Split the text into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Create a frequency dictionary\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddea Example Outputs:\n\n```python\nword_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\") == {\"hello\": 2, \"world\": 1}\nword_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\") == {\"python\": 2, \"is\": 2, \"great\": 1, \"fun\": 1}\nword_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\") == {\"test\": 3}\n```\n\n---\n\nLet me know if you'd like a version that handles apostrophes (e.g., \"don't\") or other special cases!",
